; A nil tail under a declared non-nil return used to be accepted silently, and
; the function returned 0 -- while every OTHER wrong tail was already rejected
; ("hello" -> TUR-E0709, 1.5 -> TUR-E0707, true -> TUR-E0709).  The dispatcher's
; predicates are each targeted at one confusable PAIR of types, and nil had no
; predicate at all.
;
; The tail here is a nil literal reached through a multi-form body, because that
; is the shape a missing close paren leaves behind -- and because the single-form
; and multi-form cases must behave identically.
;
; See docs/archive/nil-tail-not-checked-against-declared-return.md.
(defn f [] : int
  (println "work")
  nil)

(defn main [] : int (f))
